<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Balla Fasséké</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Balla Fasseke</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Balla Fasséké is, in the oral tradition of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-sundiata">Epic of Sundiata</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griots">jeli</a> (griot) of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundiata-keita">Sundiata Keita</a> and the ancestor of the Kouyate line of Malinke griots. Griots were the aristocratic oral historians who attended kings, recording and recalling the legacies of kings and kingdoms. In the epic, the dying king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghan-kon-fatta">Naré Maghann Konaté</a> gives Balla Fasséké, the son of his own jeli, to the young Sundiata as a gift, honouring a prophecy that Sundiata would be <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>'s greatest king. When Sassouma Berete takes control of the throne for her son Dankaran Touman, she sends Balla Fasséké and Sundiata's half-sister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanaya">Nana</a> to the sorcerer king Soumaoro Kante of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sosso-empire">Sosso</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At court the griot was tutor to the princes, master of ceremonies and adviser to the mansa, and in Mande memory Sundiata’s own griot, <b>Balla Fasséké</b>, founded the Kouyaté line of griots that still exists.</blockquote><a href="/societies/mali-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Mali Empire</a></li></ul></section>
